findings.desc:
bounded: true is not a property of the measured result, contrary to the doc comment's own claim ("bounded=true is now a property of the result rather than of the loops having run", validation-executor.ts ~L142) and the smoke test's own assertion message ("the bound is ENFORCED, not merely declared", validation-evidence-buffer-smoke.ts:213). By construction: boundValidationEvidenceOutput returns `bounded: true` as a hardcoded literal (L480) on every path that enters the reduction branch, regardless of whether the final output actually satisfies the bound. The function DOES measure `output` after each stage (the `Buffer.byteLength(output,'utf8') >= VALIDATION_EVIDENCE_BOUND_BYTES` checks at L452/L464/L472, each gating escalation to the next stage including the verdictOnlyRecord fallback), but that final measurement (`serializedBytes` at L475) is never used to set `bounded`, throw, or otherwise fail loudly when the postcondition still does not hold after the last resort. The measurement is taken but not enforced.

This is not theoretical. The postcondition genuinely fails for an input inside the function's own documented general contract ("bulk in a field added later is bounded too", L313, for `result: unknown`). `droppedKeys` -- used mid-function (L444, L456) and by verdictOnlyRecord (L520, the guaranteed last resort) -- stores the RAW, untruncated top-level KEY NAME string of every field it drops. `droppedKeys.slice(0, 20)` caps the ARRAY to 20 entries but never caps each entry's own string length. Every existing test (smoke sections 8, 8b, 8b-ii) puts bulk in a field's VALUE ('someFieldAddedLater': 'z'.repeat(30*MIB); summary/provider as huge strings; summary as a huge array) -- never in the KEY NAME -- so this gap is untested.

Reproduced against the built dist (packages/main/dist/core/validation-executor.js, locally rebuilt 2026-08-15T12:55, after HEAD 29968050ff / 2365be5586):
  const hugeKeyName = 'x'.repeat(30_000_000);
  const result = { [hugeKeyName]: 'small', status: 'pass', allPass: true };
  const bounded = boundValidationEvidenceOutput(result);
  // bounded.bounded === true
  // Buffer.byteLength(bounded.output, 'utf8') === 30000284
  // VALIDATION_EVIDENCE_BOUND_BYTES === 25165824  (30000284 >= 25165824: the "GUARANTEED... strictly smaller" claim does not hold)
A second fixture (30 top-level fields, each with a ~2,000,000-char key name and a 1-char value) produces a 40,000,438-byte output, also reported bounded:true. In both cases the reduction reaches verdictOnlyRecord -- whose own docstring (L502-505) claims "a fixed upper bound of a few hundred bytes no matter what arrives" -- and that record's droppedKeys.slice(0,20) re-embeds the same unbounded key-name bulk it was supposed to eliminate, so even the guaranteed fallback is not size-independent of the input.

Reachability, stated precisely rather than overclaimed: the one production call site (handlers/verifier.ts:842, persistStoryValidationEvidence -> boundValidationEvidenceOutput(result)) always passes an object whose top-level keys are fixed TS-typed literal property names (ValidationResult's own fields plus disposition/commandExecutions/etc.), never derived from validate-command content, so this exact vector is not reachable through today's only wired caller. But boundValidationEvidenceOutput is exported and typed `result: unknown`, its own doc comment claims generality over "the whole object" (not "the whole object provided its own key names stay short"), and the `bounded` flag it returns is unconditional regardless of caller. A future caller, or a refactor of the current one that ever spreads externally-sourced keys onto the top-level object, inherits a false "enforced" guarantee with neither test coverage nor runtime enforcement to catch it -- the same defect family (an absolute invariant claim that does not hold for an unmodeled shape) that F-001 was filed against, now relocated to the KEY dimension instead of the VALUE dimension, and additionally hidden by a `bounded` flag that cannot ever report false.

Recommend: (1) derive `bounded` from the actual final measurement (`Buffer.byteLength(output,'utf8') < VALIDATION_EVIDENCE_BOUND_BYTES`) rather than hardcoding it once the branch is entered, so a future gap in the reduction strategy is visible in the return value instead of asserted away; (2) cap each droppedKeys entry's own string length (not just the array length) in both the main function and verdictOnlyRecord, since a dropped field's NAME is exactly the kind of caller-controlled string the rest of this story already treats as untrusted when it appears as a VALUE.
